1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,950
A four-by-four FrozenLake grid appears —
sixteen tiles of ice.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,950
The bottom-right tile is the goal.
Four dark holes wait between here and there.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,950
The elf starts at the top-left corner
and steps right — then right again, then down.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,950
The ice is slippery.
The elf lands on a hole and vanishes.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,950
It returns to the start, having learned nothing yet —
only that one path failed.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,950
A second attempt: the elf steps down
three tiles in a row.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,950
A different hole. The same outcome.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,950
No one tells the elf which moves are safe —
it can only observe what happens.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,950
On the third attempt,
the elf finds a different path.

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,950
It reaches the goal tile.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,950
A single number appears: plus one.
That is the reward — the signal that this outcome was good.

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,950
This is the reinforcement learning problem:
learn to act by interacting — no labels, just rewards.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,950
Three learning paradigms side by side.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,950
Supervised learning trains from labeled examples:
an input paired with the correct answer.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,950
Unsupervised learning finds hidden structure in data —
no labels and no rewards.

16
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,950
Reinforcement learning is different from both.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,950
The agent receives no labels.
There is no supervisor. Only a reward signal.

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,950
The agent must discover which actions are good —
the supervisor does not exist.

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,950
The Agent — the decision-maker in the loop.

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,950
The Environment —
everything outside the agent.

21
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,950
The elf navigating FrozenLake is an agent.
The grid itself is the environment.

22
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,950
The agent chooses an Action —
one direction to step on each time step.

23
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,950
The environment sends back a State —
what the agent perceives after acting.

24
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,950
In FrozenLake, the elf sees exactly which tile it occupies.
That is its state.

25
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,950
The environment also sends a Reward —
a single number signaling how well the last action went.

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,950
In FrozenLake that reward is one at the goal
and zero everywhere else.

27
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,950
The loop is now complete: Agent and Environment,
connected by Action, State, and Reward.

28
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,950
Not every environment reveals its full state —
the elf is an unusually transparent case.

29
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,950
At every time step: the agent
observes the state.

30
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,950
It chooses an action.

31
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,950
The environment reacts and returns
a new state and a reward.

32
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,950
Then the agent observes again —
and the cycle continues.

33
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:01,950
This repeats on every time step,
for the length of the entire episode.

34
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,950
Two paths on the same grid —
one successful, one not.

35
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,950
The green trail reaches the goal
and collects a reward of one.

36
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,950
The red trail ends at a hole —
reward zero.

37
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,950
The agent's goal is to accumulate reward
across the whole episode — not just survive the next step.

38
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,950
Rewards can be positive, zero, or negative —
the exact values depend on the environment.

39
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,950
The first distinguishing feature
of reinforcement learning: Trial-and-Error Search.

40
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,950
The agent must explore — no supervisor labels
which action is correct.

41
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,950
The second distinguishing feature: Delayed Reward.

42
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,950
An action now may affect rewards
many steps later.

43
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,200
The agent also faces a tension —
try known good actions, or explore new ones.

44
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:37,950
We will return to this.

45
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,950
Reinforcement learning is the science of learning
to act by trial and error — no labels, just rewards.

46
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,950
The Agent, the Environment, the loop —
that is reinforcement learning.

47
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,500
In the next video, we give this loop a precise
mathematical home: the Markov Decision Process.
